WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:02.487
A malicious page can make your browser send

2
00:00:02.487 --> 00:00:03.945
a request to your bank.

3
00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:05.470
We'll see why that works,

4
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:08.900
and how the SameSite attribute closes the hole.

5
00:00:09.345 --> 00:00:10.895
You log in to the bank.

6
00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:12.632
It sets a session cookie,

7
00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:14.550
and from then on the browser sends that

8
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.325
cookie with every request to the bank.

9
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.282
Then you open another site.

10
00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:21.562
That page contains a hidden form aimed at

11
00:00:21.562 --> 00:00:22.825
the bank.

12
00:00:22.945 --> 00:00:24.275
The browser submits it,

13
00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:25.720
and attaches your cookie,

14
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.283
because the request is going to the bank.

15
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:30.550
The bank sees a valid session and moves

16
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:31.875
the money.

17
00:00:32.295 --> 00:00:35.120
The first condition is a cookie based session.

18
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.620
The victim's browser supplies the credentials,

19
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.450
not the attacker.

20
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.800
Second, a request whose every parameter the attacker

21
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.845
can predict.

22
00:00:43.845 --> 00:00:45.450
The bank can't tell it from a real

23
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.575
one.

24
00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:48.045
Third, one visit.

25
00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:50.307
The attacker never reads the response.

26
00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:52.500
A side effect is enough.

27
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.995
The form's action points at the bank,

28
00:00:54.995 --> 00:00:57.475
not at the attacker's own server.

29
00:00:57.645 --> 00:01:00.875
The fields are exactly what the bank expects.

30
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.675
And the script submits it the moment the

31
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:03.770
page loads.

32
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.450
You see nothing.

33
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.545
SameSite is about sites,

34
00:01:07.545 --> 00:01:08.808
not origins.

35
00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:11.125
A request from evil.com to the bank is

36
00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:12.600
cross-site.

37
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.800
But two subdomains of the same registrable domain

38
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.395
count as the same site,

39
00:01:17.395 --> 00:01:19.675
and so do different ports.

40
00:01:20.095 --> 00:01:22.075
With Strict the cookie never travels on a

41
00:01:22.075 --> 00:01:24.783
cross-site request, even when you click a link.

42
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.000
You arrive logged out.

43
00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:29.863
Lax sends it on top-level navigations that only

44
00:01:29.863 --> 00:01:31.720
read, like following a link,

45
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.982
but withholds it on forged POSTs,

46
00:01:33.982 --> 00:01:37.075
form posts and subresource loads.

47
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.470
None restores the old behaviour,

48
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.675
and browsers require the Secure flag with it.

49
00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:44.970
The attack starts the same way.

50
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.850
You visit the page and the form fires.

51
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.770
But the cookie is marked SameSite=Lax,

52
00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.782
and this is a cross-site POST.

53
00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:55.225
So the request goes out bare.

54
00:01:55.345 --> 00:01:57.620
The bank sees no session and refuses.

55
00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.650
The forgery fails.

56
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.350
SameSite=Lax is the part that stops forged POSTs.

57
00:02:04.495 --> 00:02:07.383
Secure and HttpOnly are separate:

58
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:10.975
HTTPS only, and hidden from scripts.

59
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.170
Lax still sends cookies on top-level GET navigations,

60
00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.925
so a state-changing GET stays exploitable.

61
00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.470
A compromised subdomain is same-site,

62
00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.975
so the browser sends the cookie.

63
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.345
That's why CSRF tokens remain worth adding:

64
00:02:27.345 --> 00:02:29.662
the attacker cannot read the secret from another

65
00:02:29.662 --> 00:02:30.875
site.

66
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.800
Which of these loses its cookie?

67
00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:38.837
It's B. The link click is a safe

68
00:02:38.837 --> 00:02:41.895
top-level navigation, so Lax allows it.

69
00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:44.182
And the subdomain request is same-site,

70
00:02:44.182 --> 00:02:46.925
so even Strict sends the cookie.
